        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best video doorbell overall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most households the Ring Battery Video Doorbell Plus is the safest default because it runs on battery or wiring, offers a taller head-to-toe view and has the biggest accessory ecosystem. If you live in Google Home, the Nest Doorbell is the more natural fit, and if you want to avoid fees, the Eufy E340 deserves a close look.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do video doorbells require a subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not to work at a basic level. Most still send alerts and allow live view without a plan, but saving clips, longer history and smarter detection are often tied to a monthly or annual plan. Plan terms change, so check the current details on the manufacturer's page, or choose a model with local storage to avoid recurring fees.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a wired or battery doorbell better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you have a working doorbell circuit, wired is better because it never needs charging. Battery models suit renters and homes without wiring, at the cost of periodic recharges. Hybrid models such as the Ring Battery Plus and the Nest Doorbell let you use house power with the battery as backup.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a video doorbell myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes. Battery models need only a few screws, and wired models need basic low-voltage work after you switch off the breaker. Check your transformer rating and chime compatibility first. If you are uncomfortable working near the transformer or panel, hire a licensed electrician for that part.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a video doorbell work with my existing chime?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, but not always. Mechanical chimes usually need a small power kit or adapter, and digital chimes can need a bypass. Most manufacturers publish a compatibility checker, and several include the kit in the box. Check the support page for your doorbell and your chime type before you order.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
